": 156, "canonicalModel": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "firstParty", "costBasis": "list"}}, "claude_code_version": "2.1.289", "debrief": "Two things were awkward. Neither blocked me.\n\n1. **Challenging only part of a memory (`challenge`).** The subscription-review memory makes two claims. CODEOWNERS contradicts the first one: billing-core is no longer the reviewing team. Nothing I saw confirms or refutes the second: Lena Fischer approves plan and price changes. `challenge` only flags the whole memory, so the Lena part now looks disputed too. I would have wanted either a way to name which claim is being challenged, or a `split` operation that turns one memory into two. Then I could challenge the ownership claim, `update` it to @acme/revenue-eng (REV-7), and leave the Lena claim unflagged until someone confirms it. `update` couldn't do this cleanly, because rewriting the content would mean asserting something about the Lena rule that I didn't know.\n\n2. **Dates missing from the injected context.** The rule for `challenge` depends on whether the contradicting source is clearly newer than the memory. The memories injected by the hook showed no `created_at` or `valid_from`. I inferred the memory was older because CODEOWNERS dates the move to 2026-09-22, but I didn't check. A `get` call would have told me, so this was extra work rather than something I couldn't do. Including the date in each injected entry would have made that check immediate.", "debrief_cost_usd": 0.28549119999999995, "retries": 0}, "rep": 0}
