"2.1.289", "debrief": "Three things were missing or awkward:\n\n1. **A valid-until date on `update` / `create` (alongside the existing `valid_from`).** The UTC memory wasn't simply wrong. It's still true for report months before 2026-09 and false from then on. I wanted to close its validity at 2026-09 and add a superseding \"America/New_York from 2026-09 (REP-19)\" memory that starts there. `valid_from` only sets a start date. The other option, `resolve` with invalidate, would have thrown away the part that's still true for older months. So I used `challenge`, which just marks the whole memory as doubtful. To be fair, I could have used `create` with `supersedes` and `valid_from` for the new rule, and I didn't. That part was my omission, not a gap in the tools.\n\n2. **A scope or \"partial\" option on `challenge`.** I wanted to say \"outdated from 2026-09 on, still right before that.\" Without it, anyone reading the flag may assume the old UTC cut is wrong everywhere.\n\n3. **Dates in the context the hooks inject.** The UserPromptSubmit hook showed \"Report months are UTC months\" with no created or last-verified date. The rules ask me to decide whether a source is \"clearly newer\" than a memory. With no date on the memory, I could only guess against the docstring's \"since the 2026-09 close\". Showing `created_at` / `valid_from` next to each injected memory would have let me make that call from evidence, not inference.", "debrief_cost_usd": 0.34647, "retries": 0}, "rep": 0}
